variational-expansion-240d9c73.conceptual_definition:
The Variational Expansion formula, derived from the principle of stationary action, quantifies how a change in the path of a system (
\delta q
) affects the total action integral (
\int L dt
). It shows that the first-order variation of the action is given by an integral involving the partial derivatives of the Lagrangian (
L
) with respect to the generalized coordinates (
q
) and their time derivatives (
\dot{q}
), multiplied by the variations of those quantities (
\delta q
 and 
\delta \dot{q}
). This expansion is the foundational step in deriving the Euler-Lagrange equations of motion.
